mpc2000xl: tune desktop DATA wheel keys, drag and scroll

F14/F15 made the DATA wheel unreachable on an ordinary ANSI keyboard. F9
and F10 are not substitutes here: this same matrix already assigns them to
16 Levels and Next Seq. Put decrement/increment on minus/equals, which are
free and adjacent on that keyboard.

A relative analog field applies PORT_KEYDELTA on every input frame while a
digital direction is held, then applies PORT_SENSITIVITY to the accumulated
field. The original sensitivity 100/key delta 1 therefore cannot make both a
short key tap exactly one detent and a hold repeat slowly. Keep mouse motion
on the low byte, and put the two keyboard directions on spare high bits. The
existing 60 Hz panel dial timer gives an edge one detent, waits 300 ms, then
repeats every six ticks (10 detents/second). Add the keyboard offset to both
the accurate panel and optional panel-HLE reads, and save it with the machine.

A wheel notch rides its own small IPT_DIAL field, so notch delivery keeps
the dial-device mouse path (mouse switches on non-dial fields are only
delivered under the global -mouse option, which hides and captures the
pointer). One notch is exactly one detent, whatever the drag sensitivity
is set to; keeping notches on the drag field's key delta made every drag
retune silently change the notch too.

The drag is gated on the left mouse button. X11 only delivers relative
axis motion while a button is held, so the raw axis looked correctly gated
there, but Wayland delivers it whenever the pointer moves: the wheel turned
on every pass across the window, recorded no click at all, and kept turning
after a drag released outside the window - the "stuck drag" report. The
gate bit is asserted by an invisible full-panel artwork element, the same
mechanism the device-menu bar uses, because it is the one mouse-button
path that does not need -mouse. The timer folds the axis into a drag
accumulator only while that bit is set, on every platform; the wheel and
the keyboard work without the button, only the drag asks for it.

Use sensitivity 7 so a slow 100-pixel vertical drag gives about seven
detents; at the previous 20 the drag read as roughly three times too fast.

The artwork's raw input tag made render_target::update_pointer_fields()
call set_value(1) while the marker was held, overriding relative input,
and the layout helper then wrote user_value, which ioport_field::
set_user_settings() only applies to adjusters and setting lists, not
analog dials. Both broken paths are gone, and with them the decorative
three-digit counter in the wheel's centre: after the port became a
relative dial it could only ever show the transient accumulator, so it
answered nothing. Remove the element rather than keep a number that
lies.

Measured on the MAIN screen Sq field with a private Xvfb, LCD export,
private configuration, and the same focused build. Keyboard: an exact
50 ms emulated key press moves one step; a two-second hold moves 17
steps in total (the edge plus steady 10/second repeat after 300 ms).
Scroll: each notch moves exactly one step in both directions. Drag:
with the button held, a slow 50-pixel drag moves 3-4 steps (~7 per 100
pixels); with no button, and with only the middle button held, vertical
motion moves nothing. All values were captured in one run of the same
binary.

A binding-table collision check also found that A drove both Step < and
Undo, and S drove both Step > and Erase. Keep A/S on the step pair and
move the two editing controls to the previously unused Backspace/Delete
keys so every key has one unambiguous panel action.
